 "metadata": {
  "tags": "Industrial electronica, darksynth; vast negative space and cold, restrained mix; 138 BPM half-time stride with saturated detuned analog sub-bass, dry crushing kick, cavernous snare, ticking metallic percussion, and cold granular pads; low intimate baritone, clipped rhythmic staccato talk-singing, dry and up-front, entering within seconds; stripped bridge, short natural ending.",
  "prompt": "[Intro]\n\n[Verse 1]\nLights out on the ninetieth floor\nNobody lives here anymore\nBut the stairwell hums the way you climbed\nOne step heavy, one step light\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nI put my hand against the wall\nIt's warm, it's warm, it knew us all\n\n[Chorus]\nYou walked these halls a hundred years ago\nYour footsteps poured into the floor\nThe stone kept every word you said\nIt kept the shape of every door\nThe concrete remembers your weight\nThe girders remember your hand\nEverything you built is still awake\nStill waiting for you to stand\n\n[Verse 2]\nRain finds the cracks you used to fill\nThe elevator's waiting still\nA coat hook holds the shape of you\nA window holds the evening blue\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nI put my hand against the wall\nIt's warm, it's warm, it knew us all\n\n[Chorus]\nYou walked these halls a hundred years ago\nYour footsteps poured into the floor\nThe stone kept every word you said\nIt kept the shape of every door\nThe concrete remembers your weight\nThe girders remember your hand\nEverything you built is still awake\nStill waiting for you to stand\n\n[Bridge]\nNothing here forgets\nNothing here forgets\nPour me in the foundation\nLet me hold the next\n\n[Final Chorus]\nThe concrete remembers your weight\nThe girders remember your hand\nEverything you built is still awake\nStill waiting for you to stand\n\n[Outro]\nNothing here forgets",
